  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Thu May 14 2026\n\nVery stagnant weather pattern Friday through the weekend which \nwill be consistently sunny, warm, and breezy each day. Longwave \ntrough pattern across the Western U.S. with the primary jet flow \nto our north will lead to mostly benign conditions outside of fire\nweather concerns. Next couple days will feature warm, typical\nmid-May temperatures with lowland highs in the upper 80s/lower\n90s. Southwest winds increasing during the afternoon hours 15 to\n25 mph.\n\nBase of the trough swings across the Southern Rockies Sunday and \nMonday, causing the jet stream to dip south over our area and \nincrease winds. Southwest winds 25 to 35 mph still remain below \nAdvisory criteria. A subtle cool down in temperatures will also \noccur, with lowland highs in the mid-to-upper 80s. No \nprecipitation expected over the next 7 days as conditions are \nextremely dry.\n\nNegatively tiled ridge over the area late next week will allow \nfor lighter winds locally. Both GFS and ECMWF show a cold front \nout of the plains around the Thursday/Friday timeframe. While this\nwon't affect temperatures much, surface high pressure behind the \nboundary could lead to a dryline intrusion back into the area. \nChances for showers and thunderstorms would return to the forecast\nduring the May 22-25 timeframe if this occurs.",
